  breadcrumbLabel="Marine Turbocharger Identification Guide"
>
  <p>Marine turbochargers can look similar even when their internal parts, housings, mounting arrangements or operating requirements are different. Before requesting a complete replacement unit, cartridge, rotor, nozzle ring, bearing or other turbocharger component, collect the identification details from the existing unit and the engine it serves.</p>

  <p>The goal is not to guess a match from appearance. The goal is to give enough evidence for the requested turbocharger or part to be checked against the correct maker, model and application.</p>

  <h2>1. Start with the turbocharger nameplate</h2>
  <p>The nameplate is usually the most useful source of identification information. Photograph it straight-on in good light and make sure every line is readable.</p>
  <p>Record any of the following that are shown:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Turbocharger maker or brand</li>
    <li>Model or type designation</li>
    <li>Serial number</li>
    <li>Assembly or specification number</li>
    <li>Manufacturing or licence information</li>
    <li>Any stamped or engraved reference codes</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Keep the full model exactly as written. Letters, suffixes and size codes can distinguish different versions within the same turbocharger family.</p>

  <h2>2. Record the engine maker and full engine model</h2>
  <p>A turbocharger quotation should not rely on the turbocharger model alone. Include the engine maker and complete engine model because the same turbocharger family may be configured differently for different engines.</p>
  <p>Also include the engine serial number when available. If the unit is installed on a generator or auxiliary engine, state that application clearly.</p>

  <h2>3. Photograph both compressor and turbine sides</h2>
  <p>Take clear photos of the complete turbocharger from several angles. Useful views include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Compressor inlet and outlet</li>
    <li>Turbine inlet and exhaust outlet</li>
    <li>Bearing housing</li>
    <li>Mounting feet or flange</li>
    <li>Oil inlet and drain connections</li>
    <li>Cooling-water connections if present</li>
    <li>Any actuator, wastegate or control linkage if fitted</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These photos help confirm the physical arrangement and can reveal whether the requested item is a complete turbocharger, a core assembly or a specific service part.</p>

  <h2>4. Look for part numbers on the component being replaced</h2>
  <p>If the requirement is for a turbocharger part rather than the complete unit, inspect the old component for stamped, cast or engraved numbers. A part number on the damaged item can be more precise than a general description such as “bearing,” “seal” or “nozzle ring.”</p>

  <p>Do not remove labels or grind surfaces to expose markings. Clean only enough to make existing identification readable.</p>

  <h2>5. Include measurements only when they are useful</h2>
  <p>Measurements can support identification when a plate or part number is missing, but they should not replace a known maker/model reference.</p>
  <p>Depending on the request, useful measurements may include flange dimensions, bolt-hole centres, inlet/outlet diameter, shaft dimensions or the overall size of the removed component.</p>

  <h2>6. State what you actually need</h2>
  <p>Be specific about the scope of the enquiry. For example:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Complete turbocharger</li>
    <li>Cartridge or core assembly</li>
    <li>Rotor or shaft assembly</li>
    <li>Nozzle ring</li>
    <li>Bearings and seals</li>
    <li>Compressor wheel</li>
    <li>Turbine wheel</li>
    <li>Gasket or service kit</li>
  </ul>

  <p>If you are unsure, describe the failure and attach photos rather than naming a part from memory.</p>

  <h2>7. Describe the condition and failure symptoms</h2>
  <p>Useful symptoms can include abnormal noise, oil leakage, excessive shaft movement, damaged blades, smoke, reduced boost or visible housing damage. These symptoms do not by themselves identify the correct replacement, but they provide context for the enquiry.</p>

  <p>Do not assume that a symptom proves a specific internal failure. Inspection and measurement are still required.</p>

  <h2>8. Include the required condition</h2>
  <p>State whether you are looking for a new, genuine, OEM, reconditioned or serviceable used unit or part. Availability and exact condition must be confirmed for the specific model at the time of quotation.</p>

  <h2>9. Send a complete identification package</h2>
  <div class="checklist">
    <p><strong>Turbocharger maker:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Turbocharger model/type:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Serial / assembly number:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Engine maker:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Engine model:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Engine serial number:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Required part or complete unit:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Required condition:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Quantity:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Destination:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Nameplate and unit photos attached:</strong> Yes / No</p>
  </div>

